WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.931
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.231 --> 00:00:09.768
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.068 --> 00:00:16.203
Unit twenty: Travel Writing. Today you will order several adjectives correctly in vivid descriptions.

4
00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:19.121
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.300
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.221
Travel writing.

7
00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:30.979
Travel writing means writing that describes journeys, places and experiences.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:34.245
Travel writing can be both factual and personal.

9
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.328
Your turn. Say: Travel writing.

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:41.470
Travel writing.

11
00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:42.916
Journey.

12
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:46.357
Journey means a trip from one place to another.

13
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.089
The journey took three days by boat.

14
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:51.720
Your turn. Say: Journey.

15
00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:55.289
Journey.

16
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:57.132
Description.

17
00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:01.614
Description means words that tell what someone or something is like.

18
00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:04.576
The description made me feel I was there.

19
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:08.114
Your turn. Say: Description.

20
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:12.142
Description.

21
00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:13.803
Culture.

22
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:18.221
Culture means the customs, beliefs and art of a group of people.

23
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:21.072
She wrote about Khmer culture with respect.

24
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:24.022
Your turn. Say: Culture.

25
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:27.740
Culture.

26
00:01:28.483 --> 00:01:29.438
Narrative.

27
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.193
Narrative means an account of events told as a story.

28
00:01:33.783 --> 00:01:36.236
The narrative follows one traveller's year.

29
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.003
Your turn. Say: Narrative.

30
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:42.937
Narrative.

31
00:01:43.683 --> 00:01:44.835
Observation.

32
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.730
Observation means careful noticing of what is seen, heard or felt.

33
00:01:50.317 --> 00:01:53.082
A sharp observation can bring a street to life.

34
00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:56.835
Your turn. Say: Observation.

35
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:00.922
Observation.

36
00:02:01.683 --> 00:02:02.510
Place.

37
00:02:03.517 --> 00:02:07.246
Place means a particular town, region or location.

38
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.780
Every place has its own rhythm.

39
00:02:10.383 --> 00:02:12.885
Your turn. Say: Place.

40
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:16.514
Place.

41
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.309
Perspective.

42
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:22.408
Perspective means the point of view from which something is seen.

43
00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:25.632
A local perspective adds depth to the story.

44
00:02:26.217 --> 00:02:28.904
Your turn. Say: Perspective.

45
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:32.907
Perspective.

46
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.490
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:44.520
Visal, a writer describes 'a small old wooden fishing boat'. Why not 'a wooden old small fishing boat'?

48
00:02:45.617 --> 00:03:00.378
English uses a usual adjective order: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose, then the noun. So: a lovely small old round red Thai wooden fishing boat.

49
00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:02.984
That is very long!

50
00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:18.450
Yes, so writers normally use two or three. Use commas between adjectives of the same type, like 'a warm, friendly welcome', but not between different types: a small wooden boat.

51
00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.097
Does order affect meaning?

52
00:03:22.183 --> 00:03:34.626
It affects naturalness. Wrong order sounds strange to native readers. Opinion words come first because they show the writer's feelings: a charming narrow cobbled lane.

53
00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:37.662
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:45.032
Grammar focus: Adjective order: opinion – size – age – colour – material plus noun.

55
00:03:45.332 --> 00:03:46.485
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.183 --> 00:03:49.868
We sailed in a small old wooden fishing boat.

57
00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:53.270
She walked down a charming narrow cobbled lane.

58
00:03:54.183 --> 00:03:56.845
He wore a beautiful long red silk scarf.

59
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:08.187
Order adjectives: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose, then the noun. Use two or three at most. Commas go between adjectives of the same type.

60
00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:13.007
Be careful! Don't say: She wore a silk red beautiful scarf.

61
00:04:14.967 --> 00:04:18.046
Say: She wore a beautiful red silk scarf.

62
00:04:18.346 --> 00:04:20.508
Use opinion, colour, material order.

63
00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.952
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:26.441
Writing About Places.

65
00:04:26.741 --> 00:04:30.714
Good travel writing begins with careful observation of a place and its people.

66
00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.869
A vivid description might mention a narrow old wooden bridge over a wide brown river.

67
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:42.444
The journey becomes a narrative when the writer shows how each place changed their perspective.

68
00:04:43.433 --> 00:04:48.347
A writer who respects local culture avoids stereotypes and listens before judging.

69
00:04:49.333 --> 00:04:53.063
A lovely, quiet morning market can teach more than a long guidebook.

70
00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:58.261
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:59.117 --> 00:05:00.675
She bought a blank scarf.

72
00:05:00.975 --> 00:05:05.685
A, beautiful silk. B, silk beautiful. C, silk and beautiful.

73
00:05:09.233 --> 00:05:11.546
The answer is A: beautiful silk.

74
00:05:12.683 --> 00:05:16.169
Careful noticing of what is seen, heard or felt is blank.

75
00:05:16.469 --> 00:05:20.714
A, perspective. B, observation. C, narrative.

76
00:05:24.267 --> 00:05:26.452
The answer is B: observation.

77
00:05:27.617 --> 00:05:29.709
Which phrase has the usual order?

78
00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:35.160
A, a wooden old table. B, an old wooden table. C, a wooden table old.

79
00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.920
The answer is B: an old wooden table.

80
00:05:42.083 --> 00:05:44.629
A trip from one place to another is a blank.

81
00:05:44.929 --> 00:05:47.781
A, culture. B, journey. C, lane.

82
00:05:51.333 --> 00:05:53.205
The answer is B: journey.

83
00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.499
According to the reading, what should a writer do before judging?

84
00:05:57.799 --> 00:06:02.822
A, listen to local people. B, buy souvenirs. C, write a guidebook.

85
00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:09.411
The answer is A: listen to local people.

86
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:13.810
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.893
Which place would you most like to write about?

88
00:06:25.683 --> 00:06:27.288
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:27.588 --> 00:06:45.966
I would most like to write about the old town in my own province. It has narrow, quiet streets, small wooden houses and a busy morning market. I want to describe the smells, the sounds and the kind people. My local perspective could show readers a place that guidebooks often forget.

90
00:06:46.266 --> 00:06:48.091
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:48.983 --> 00:06:56.849
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and adjective order: opinion – size – age – colour – material plus noun.

92
00:06:57.149 --> 00:07:02.529
Today's value is global awareness. Remember that every story is connected to the wider world.

93
00:07:02.829 --> 00:07:05.749
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:07:06.049 --> 00:07:08.585
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

